A||||18||LZeitgeist||Fiero towing possibilities... advice needed, please...||||1||
Z||000000||LZeitgeist||04-21-2001||07:49 PM||||O.K., here's the situation...<P>I've got an '88 Formula, and I'd like to put a trailer hitch on it to be able to tow an '88 Coupe behind it for short trips (100 miles or less, once or maybe twice a year). Both cars have 5-speed transmissions.<P>Here are the caveats:<P>1. I would prefer that the trailer hitch be as 'visually unobtrusive' as possible. I don't mind if there are parts still attached to the undercarriage, as long as they are not readily visible when the hitch sleeve is removed, or better yet, see Caveat #2.<P>2. Is it possible to attach the hitch in such a way that it extends through a hole cut through the rear fascia, *behind* the license plate, so that when towing, the license plate could simply be displayed in the back window if the Formula, and when not towing, the license plate would cover the hole where the hitch was inserted into the chassis-mounted brackets? This would be the idea situation in my mind, as no part of the hitch would be visible when not towing.<P>3. I'm picturing using a small tow dolly to pull the Coupe, with the other two wheels on the car resting on the ground. Is it possible to use this method to tow the car pointing frontwards, with the rear wheels resting on the ground? Would this damage the transmission (making sure, of course, that the car is in neutral with the parking brake released)? (I've heard the old wives' tale that it won't be good for the tranny, but I need facts about this... since the tranny turns when the engine drives it, how is it different if the axles are turning it instead of the engine?) <P>4. If towing the Coupe frontwards is not possible without damage, then what is the best method for securing the front wheels in a straight-forward position, besides the steering wheel lock (which I've heard should NOT be relied on for towing purposes)... is there a "Club"-like tool for locking the steering wheel in position?<P>5. Advice is greatly appreciated, especially from such salvage-yard gurus as Ed Parks, et.al... <P>Thanks in advance.<p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 04-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-21-2001||08:05 PM||||To got up the list backwards......<P>there are steering lock tools used by the towing operators. They hook to the steering wheel spokes and anchor to the seat hardware.<P>You can make this tool<P>bend/cut steel to a Y shape that will hook over the spokes. Rebar is good for this but you will have to heat it to bend it. pu a section of garden hose on the hooks to protect the steering wheel.<P>put a section of pipe under the seat that will reach across the seat rails. You want the longest pipe you can fit so it cant slide sideways and pop out.<P>connect the Y and pipe with a ratcheting cargo strap and suck it up tight. WARNING! TILT Steering must be set ALL THE WAY DOWN when using the lock. Other wise the tilt lock can slip and cuase the lock to go slack.<P>A manual tanny is less of a towing issue than an auto.<P>The hitch<P>Unless you have a very experienced fabricator on hand I would not attempt to make one. Problems that arise from home made hitches can have fatal results. (And yest I've actually seen hitch failures. Not pretty.)<P>The ones I've seen. When used on an 88. would probably look best if you remove the center section of the trim below the facial rather than cut it.<P>For sources try any autoparts store. Some U haul/HitchWorld dealers can help you as well. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||pherder||04-21-2001||08:44 PM||||I am waiting on MrFiero to add his experiences here ...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Well, since I don't see him answering at the second, I will tell you how an 86 SE (auto) arrived @ my house a couple of months ago.<P>MrFiero was driving an 88 GT with a receiver trailer hitch (removable tongue/ball) pulling a VERY heavy two wheel dolly with the 86 SE backwards on the dolly.  He drove this way from Denver, CO to ~Wichita, KS (500 miles?) at the speed limit or there abouts.<P>I know he has trailered Fieros this way a LOT of times!<P>For another example ... as you stated, Ed Parks used to drive all over the US with his Fiero pulling his somewhat lighter weight two wheel dolly and Fieros on it.  Autos have to be dragged backwards (engine drives the pump that lubes the insides, NOT the axles!) but manuals can be dragged forward (although I dragged my 88 Coupe 5 speed backwards from TX to KS because I had no lube in the transaxle.)<P>On the trailer hitch, Outlaw Performance sells a receiver hitch ( <A HREF="http://www.outlawperformance.org/main.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.outlawperformance.org/main.htm</A> ) that just shows from under the rear valance.  I bid on one they sold on eBay ($75 + $20 shipping then ... now bidding starts @ $80).  I believe that this receiver hitch is the one sold by The Fiero Store and is the one that MrFiero uses.  It is rated @ 2000 lbs. so it really shouldn't be used to tow another Fiero but ...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Paul<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 04-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||04-21-2001||09:22 PM||||Here I am!<P>I have towed a dozen or so Fieros with my '88 GT without any problems.......I now have a truck to do the hauling, but a Fiero can be used successfully.<P><BR>The hitch I have is from the Fiero Store and does not interfere with the fascia.....no need to do any cutting  With the ball part removed, you hardly notice the hitch is there.<P>I highly recommend towing Fieros (whether stick or auto) with the rear wheels on the dolly......it helps with the weight distribution. I have never had any issues with the steering lock mechanism failing or causing problems.  I have towed Fieros cross country (literally!) like this with no issues. <P><BR>My GT is/was an auto., which is a little better for towing than a stick.  Take it easy on starts so you dont burn up the clutch.  Since you said you're only using it for trips less than 100 miles, you shouldn't have any problems.<P>BTW.....I could cruise at 75 MPH without any worries (smooth as silk).||||reg||10
Z||000004||Hank is Here||04-21-2001||09:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>4. If towing the Coupe frontwards is not possible without damage, then what is the best method for securing the front wheels in a straight-forward position, besides the steering wheel lock (which I've heard should NOT be relied on for towing purposes)... is there a "Club"-like tool for locking the steering wheel in position?<BR>(edited 04-21-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A simple motorcycle strap through the steering wheel and conected to the brake pedal works well.   The other cheap trick is just to tie the seatbelt to the steering wheel <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||04-22-2001||02:20 AM||||I think you would be asking for trouble if you tow a fiero with another fiero. generally speaking you want the tow vehicle to weight more than the towed vehicle. And i dont think you will find a hitch for a fiero with a rated weight of 3000 lbs (the car plus the dolly).<P>   the fact that someone has done this several times over hundreds of miles does not make it safe. If you have to stop in a hurry, or turn shaply to avoid an accident your are going to see the towed vehicle heading off on its own with your detached trailer hitch throwing sparks the whole way. Or worst yet, the towed vehicle will throw the tow vehicle out of control.<P>  I saw the aftermath of a guy trying to tow a small trailer full of gravel with a jeep. the jeep lost control and did several sharp S curves across both lanes of the thruway (you could see the skid marks) before it tumbled through the center divider. Not a pretty sight.<P>  If you have to tow a fiero get a tow vehicle big enough for the job. Any sharp state trooper will pull you over and call the impound truck if he spots you pulling a load that weighs more than the tow vehicle.||||reg||1
Z||000006||LZeitgeist||04-23-2001||12:10 AM||||Thanks for all the suggestions/advice, guys... <P>I'm still open for more, but this gives me a good start...||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||04-23-2001||01:05 AM||||My point was not that the Fiero is an ideal tow vehicle, but that it can successfully tow if no other car is available.  <P>I have thousands, not hundreds, of miles logged towing with that GT.  I experienced no problems accelerating, stopping or manuevering.  It obviously doesn't accelerate as quickly or stop as fast, but it is comparable to the '89 Dodge Raider I now use.<P>Since LZeitgeist will only tow once or twice a year for a distance less  than 100 miles, he should not have any problems.  Like I said, I have logged many thousands of miles (over 10K) towing with my '88 GT.....a hundred miles is nothing.<P>Go for it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tom88gt||04-23-2001||09:07 AM||||I have a Fiero Store hitch on my 88 Formula.  Pretty good hitch.  But I did have to trim just a little bit on the lower facia (you will not see it unless you are under the car).  Good fit and I have towed a 5x10 utility trailer with my Harley on it.  I would not recommend towing over 1000 lbs.  Even with the trailer empty, I can really feel it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||LZeitgeist||04-23-2001||01:59 PM||||This is all really good advice... the safety advice is just as welcome as the technical advice...<P>That's one of the things I love about this forum is that you get both sides - not only *how* I should do it, but *if* I should do it...<P>Thanks for the replies so far - anyone else with more info? (I'm not opposed to the Fiero Store hitch if it meets my caveats, but I like to be open to other proven suggestions as well...)<P>EDIT: typos<p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 04-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||04-23-2001||02:54 PM||||other options to get two vehicles 100 miles away once or twice a year:<P>Get someone else interested in whatever it is you are doing, a nephew, neighbor, cousin... and let them drive the 2nd car.<P>Rent a rig that can tow both cars, ie a flatbed tow truck or double lenght trailer, I a pickup truck or SUV, once a year for the ocassion.<P>Drive one car there and take a train, bus, flight, bum a ride... then drive the otherone.<P>Wire the second car for radio control, ala Back to the future I, and drive one car with each hand  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>whatever it is you are taking the cars to every 6 months, take one the first time and the other one the second time. <P>You need to seriously consider the risks you are taking by overloading the tow capacity of the car. I checked the Climer Publication for the fiero, they rate its tow capacity at 1000 lbs max. If some stupid woodchuck or skunk darts out infront of you, causing you to instinctively nail the brakes, and you jacknife both your fieros into each other...<P>If the second car is not an autotranny you could get by with a towbar for 100 miles, but you would want to have it in your head that you are drastically exceeding the towing capibilites of the tow car, and force yourself to putt along at 50mph the whole way and sit on an old distributor cap the whole time so you dont get 'too comfortable' doing this. <P>You can get away with alot of stuff in a pinch. I towed a VW bug 60 miles one dark Sunday night using nothing but its seatbelts. (My friend showed up to rescue me when my engine threw a rod, and forgot to bring the towrope!) Once in a life for stuff like that is enough. Every 6 months?! No thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000011||LZeitgeist||04-23-2001||08:48 PM||||All good advice. <P>Here's the whole story.<P>My Dad and I just finished reworking his '88 Coupe into an Indy replica (our 1988 "Indy II", we call it...) a couple months ago, and are looking very forward to the spring and summer gatherings in and around NC, and the Nationals in Virginia in August, especially since my Formula and his "indy" are in such great shape...<P>Out of the blue, about three months ago, he, at age 76, had what the doctors are referring to as a 'mini-stroke'. He was about 30 miles from home, traveling along *very* well-known back roads when it happened, and he just *barely* made it home due to a very large degree of mental confusion, couldn't get his feet to do what he wanted them to, etc. - in short, it scared the flying crap out of him. He barely managed to drive himself home safely (which is a miracle in itself, and I'm not a particularily religious person, either...) and he got Mom to take him to the doctor.<P>There were no lasting effects that the doctors can detect - no muscle atrophy, etc. - however, when we were in Greensboro last weekend, him in the "Indy" and me in the Formula, we ended up leaving early due to him getting the beginnings of a stress-induced headache... he was becoming more and more concerned about what we would do if he started having the same troubles on the way home from Greensboro as he had when he had the stroke. <P>He feels confident driving around town again - doing errands, etc. - but the longer trip to Greensboro is what made him become more stressed about things than he normally is.<P>So, the reason I want to pursue the possibility of the tow dolly is to give him an "out"... if he knows that there is an alternative that won't screw up our traveling plans, but we can still get both cars to where we're going and back again, then he won't be worried so much about "what if"s... <P>Also, the added time and expense of bus tickets, multiple trips, etc. make those options that aren't viable for us to consider. Not bad suggestions... just not ones that will work with what we've got to work with.<P>Man, I love this place...<P><p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 04-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||LZeitgeist||04-25-2001||08:32 AM||||*bump*<P>Any other advice, especially of the technical side of things? <P>Any more gurus out there?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula88||04-25-2001||08:50 AM||||Well, we could hook both our Formulas up to one hitch in front of the Indy II and tow it Roman Chariot style!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Personally, I'd be worried about towing a Fiero with a Fiero.  Just don't know if it can do it reliably.  Don't want to risk messing one up.  As for towing on the rear wheels (5 speed). Are there any concerns?  The clutch shouldn't matter if the tranny is in neutral, right?  Wouldn't want to pull the car backwards and drag the front end on bumps.  <P>I'm also considering putting a hitch on my '99 Grand Am so I have a Fiero tow vehicle when necessary.  Now I just gotta find out what its tow rating is and what kind of hitches are available.||||reg||12
Z||000014||Wolfhound||04-25-2001||08:58 AM||||Hitch rating is tounge weight. You do not need a 3000 lb. rating unless the fiero is balanced on the ball.||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||04-25-2001||01:05 PM||||You can tow with front on dolly and rear on ground safely if its a stick. Def a nono with any automatic, radiator isnt providing any cooling to trans and itll burn up except for really short (less than 25 miles) distances.||||reg||1
Z||000016||LZeitgeist||04-25-2001||01:37 PM||||Yup, both are 5-speeds, so I'll be towing the Coupe by the front wheels. <P>It sounds do-able, albeit carefully (and sparingly). <P>Any more candidates for the hitch I should buy, keeping in mind the needs/desires in the first post?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ken Wittlief||04-25-2001||04:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wolfhound:<BR><B>Hitch rating is tounge weight. You do not need a 3000 lb. rating unless the fiero is balanced on the ball.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>NO NO NO NO NO!!!!!<P>trailer hitches are rated by tounge weight and max trailer rate. A 1000lb hitch usually has a 100lb tounge rate, 2000 lb =&gt; 200lbs on the tounge...<P>I check with uHaul to see if I could tow a Fiero on a dolly, a short distance using my new Saturn to pull it with. He said, "NFW!!!" the tow vehicle has to be at least 1000 lbs heavier than the towed vehicle pulse the weight of the tow dolly.<P>Most trailers that weigh over 1000 lbs have electic brakes. Your fiero on a dolly will be 3000lbs, and it will have NO brakes, so when you try to stop it will be like another car behind you is ramming into your rear bumper.<P>If it was this easy to tow a car, then most of us would have tow bars in our garages next to the jumper cables, but its not! Thats why garages spend all that money on tow trucks.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||LZeitgeist||04-26-2001||05:40 PM||||O.K. - I've gotten some good safety advice, and been shown that this is possible, even if it is not recommended. Advice noted from all corners, and thank you, very much.<P>However, now I need to hear from more people who have *actually done this*. I know they're out there - I know it can be done (and *has* been done), but I need to know what problems, tricks, techniques, etc have worked for people who are not just theorizing about it, but have actually towed a Fiero behind another Fiero. <P>Yes, I can visualize the dangers and the possible problems that *may* happen, but I need to base my decision on hard facts from people who have actual experience doing this. I'll be taking full and complete responsibility for my decision whether or not to do this, and for my subsequent actions, but if I'm going to do this, I'd rather do it with good, experience-backed info rather than looking at what *might* happen, because let's face it - there's a million things that *might* happen every time we get out of bed in the morning. <P>Please don't misunderstand me - I *do* appreciate the safety advice... but if I could think of a viable alternative with what we have to work with, I'd most likely prefer it. <P>Anyone else with some actual experience, either good or bad? <P>||||reg||1
